-- title:
--   Rank two for χ-eigenspaces of the corner Hecke module
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal O$ be a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with finite residue field $k=\mathrm{ResidueField}(\mathcal O)$, let $p\neq 2$ be a prime with $p\in\mathfrak m_{\mathcal O}$, and let $\bar\rho$ be a residual Galois representation over $k$ (a two-dimensional $k$-space with a $\mathrm{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$-action factoring through a finite level) which is absolutely irreducible, i.e. its base change to $\overline k$ is irreducible. Let $S,S_{\min}$ be finite sets of naturals with every element of $S$ prime, $p\in S_{\min}\subseteq S$, such that a prime $q\neq p$ lies in $S_{\min}$ exactly when $\bar\rho$ is ramified at $q$, and such that for $q\in S_{\min}$, $q\neq p$, every inertia element at $q$ has characteristic polynomial $(X-1)^2$ in $\bar\rho$. Let $N,L$ be nonzero with: every prime divisor of $N$ in $S$; $p^2\nmid N$; every $q\in S_{\min}\setminus\{p\}$ divides $N$; $q^2\mid N$ for every prime $q\neq p$ dividing $N$ but not in $S_{\min}$; $q^3\nmid N$ for all primes $q\neq p$; $L\mid N$, $L$ divisible by each prime divisor of $N$ and by $q^2$ whenever $q^2\mid N$, and $q^3\nmid L$ for all primes $q$; and assume weight-two level-$N$ cusp forms are spanned over $\mathbb C$ by the integral lattice. Let $\theta$ be a ring homomorphism from the anemic Hecke algebra $\mathbb T=\mathbb T^S(N)$ (the $\mathbb Z$-subalgebra of $\mathrm{End}_{\mathbb C}S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$ generated by the Hecke operators away from $S$) to $k$ such that for every prime $\ell\nmid N$ with $\ell\notin S$, every valuation subring $P$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $\ell$ a nonunit of $P$ and every Frobenius element $\sigma$ at $\ell$ for $P$, the characteristic polynomial of $\bar\rho(\sigma)$ is $X^2-\theta(T_\ell)X+\ell$. Let $T=\mathbb T_\theta$ be the localisation of $\mathbb T$ at the submonoid attached to $\theta$, let $M$ be a module over $T$ and over $\mathcal O$ compatibly, and let $B:M\times M\to\mathcal O$ be $\mathcal O$-bilinear, such that $(M,B)$ is a corner realisation for $(p,\bar\rho,N,L,S,\theta)$: there are a commuting family of operators indexed by the generators $\mathrm{Gen}(L,S)$, a residual system $\bar\theta$ on these generators, an idempotent splitting $Sp$ of the resulting operator subalgebra with a chosen index $i_0$ and an $\mathcal O$-algebra map from the corner ring to $k$ sending each generator to its $\bar\theta$-value, and an $\mathcal O$-linear isomorphism $e$ of $M$ with the corner submodule $e_{i_0}H^1(\Gamma_H(L),\mathcal O)$, which consists of parabolic classes; $\bar\theta(T_\ell)=\theta(T_\ell)$ for primes $\ell$ outside $S$ dividing neither $L$ nor $N$, $\bar\theta(U_q)=0$ when $q^2\mid L$, and $\bar\theta(U_p)\neq 0$ if $p\mid L$ and the adic representation attached to $\bar\rho$ is ordinary at $p$; $e$ intertwines the $T_\ell$-action on $M$ with the cohomological Hecke operator; and $B$ is the pullback under $e$ of the pairing $\mathrm{Bfam}_0$. Finally let $\mathcal O'$ be a complete discrete valuation ring of characteristic zero with finite residue field, finite over $\mathcal O$ by a local algebra map, let $\chi:T\to\mathcal O'$ be an $\mathcal O$-algebra map, and assume it is not the case that both $p\mid L$ and $\bar\rho$ fails to be ordinary at $p$. Then the intersection, over all $t\in T$, of the $\chi(t)$-eigenspaces of the $\mathcal O'$-linear endomorphism of $\mathcal O'\otimes_{\mathcal O}M$ obtained by base change from multiplication by $t$ on $M$ has $\mathcal O'$-rank exactly $2$.
--
--   This is the geometric multiplicity-two statement for the localised anemic Hecke module realised as a corner of the cohomology of a modular curve: every $\mathcal O$-algebra point $\chi$ of the local Hecke algebra has eigenlattice of rank two after base change, in the cube-free level situation met in the minimal-to-non-minimal level-raising argument. It is deduced from the corresponding count over an algebraic closure together with finiteness of the cohomology module, and feeds the comparison of the rank of the torsion submodule with twice the rank of the quotient in the study of the Hecke module as a module over the deformation ring.
